1. Refutas Equation (Viscosity Blend Index - VBI)
This equation is widely used in the petroleum and lubricant industries to estimate the resulting kinematic viscosity of blending two or more oils.
Formula to calculate the viscosity of the blend (V{blend}) in Excel:

If your Excel sheet is set up as follows:
  Cell A1: Viscosity of Oil 1 (V1).
  Cell B1: Viscosity of Oil 2 (V2).
  Cell C1: Mass fraction (or percentage expressed from 0 to 1) of Oil 1 (x1). 
  (The fraction for Oil 2 is implicitly (1 - C1)).

Paste this formula into the cell where you want the blend viscosity to appear:
Excel=EXP(EXP((($C$1*(14.534*LN(LN(A1+0.8))+10.975)+(1-$C$1)*(14.534*LN(LN(B1+0.8))+10.975))-10.975)/14.534))-0.8


2. Walther Equation (ASTM D341 Standard)This relates a fluid's kinematic viscosity to its absolute temperature and serves as the foundation for viscosity-temperature charts.

Excel formulas:To calculate the Walther function ($W$) from a known viscosity:
If the viscosity is in cell A1:Excel=LOG10(LOG10(A1 + 0.7))
To solve and calculate Viscosity (v) at a given temperature:
If you have constant $A$ in A1, constant $B$ in B1, and the temperature in degrees Celsius in C1:Excel=10^(10^(A1 - B1 * LOG10(C1 + 273.15))) - 0.7


3. Wright Equation (MacCoull-Walther-Wright Function)
An enhanced version of the Walther equation that includes an exponential correction term, 
offering greater precision when working with low viscosities (v < 2 cSt) or across wide temperature ranges.Excel formula:
If you have the kinematic viscosity (v) in cell A1, you can calculate its transformed Wright function like this:

Excel=LOG10(LOG10(A1 + 0.7 + EXP(-1.47 - 1.84*A1 - 0.51*A1^2)))
(Note: For viscosities above 2 cSt, the exponential term drops close to zero, behaving similarly to the classic Walther equation).
